explanation:
Option A is the documented conversion procedure. Local undo is a CDB-wide property. Shut down the CDB, start it in upgrade mode, issue the mode-changing statement in `CDB$ROOT`, and restart normally. Upgrade mode is the required maintenance state, not merely a restriction on ordinary user logins.

The essential sequence is:
```sql
SHUTDOWN IMMEDIATE;
STARTUP UPGRADE;
ALTER DATABASE LOCAL UNDO ON;
SHUTDOWN IMMEDIATE;
STARTUP;
```
Run this as a suitably privileged administrator after planning the maintenance outage. Oracle handles the required local-undo provisioning; the procedure does not ask you to delete the existing shared undo first.

Option B is incorrect because restricted mode is not upgrade mode and manually creating undo in every PDB is not the stated conversion procedure. Option C is incorrect because it unnecessarily drops undo and attempts a CDB property change independently within each PDB. That mixes the scope of the database mode with the storage objects used under that mode.

Option D is incorrect because read-only startup is not the required conversion state; simply reopening read/write does not substitute for the restart procedure. Option E uses upgrade mode but still issues the statement in each PDB, so its container scope is wrong.

Afterward, inspect `DATABASE_PROPERTIES` for `LOCAL_UNDO_ENABLED` and verify the intended PDB open states and undo tablespaces. Local undo allows PDB-level operations such as independent recovery and supports workflows that shared undo restricts; it does not mean each PDB runs its own Oracle instance.

With CONTROLFILE AUTOBACKUP enabled, A, B, E and F are true under the stated feature's normal prerequisites. This corrects the count to four. An explicit BACKUP SPFILE is itself a BACKUP operation and can be followed by the configured control-file autobackup; it is not an exception to the general after-backup trigger.

Option B describes the successful backup-recording trigger. Option E describes a database structural change, such as adding a tablespace, that requires updated recovery metadata. F is true when the instance uses an SPFILE: the autobackup includes it with the control file so that recovery can rebuild startup configuration as well as the control-file repository. If the instance was started using a text PFILE, RMAN cannot invent an SPFILE to include.

Option C is false: an FRA is convenient, but control-file autobackups can use other configured destinations. Option D is false because ordinary table creation is not the corresponding physical database structural-change trigger.

```text
CONFIGURE CONTROLFILE AUTOBACKUP ON;
BACKUP SPFILE;
LIST BACKUP OF CONTROLFILE;
```

Inspect the output to distinguish the explicit SPFILE backup from the additional autobackup piece. Protect the DBID and the autobackup format/location outside the database host. A current autobackup is especially valuable when all multiplexed control files are lost, because a recovery catalog or a readable current control file is not required merely to locate a correctly named control-file autobackup.

A and C are correct. ALTER SESSION SET CONTAINER changes the current container for the session; it does not commit or roll back the existing transaction. Thus the two PDB1 inserts remain uncommitted when the session switches, making A true. A session cannot perform DML in another PDB while it has an active transaction in the first PDB, so the attempted PDB2 insert fails, making C true. B and G incorrectly assume the container switch commits. E incorrectly assumes the switch itself rolls back. D invents an autonomous child transaction, and F says the second insert is merely left uncommitted even though the cross-container restriction causes it to fail. This is an operational trap for SYS sessions used in maintenance scripts: explicitly commit or roll back work before changing containers, and handle the failure before proceeding against another PDB.

A container switch changes session context; it is not a transaction boundary and does not silently commit or roll back the transaction started in PDB1. Oracle permits the switch, but while that transaction remains active the session cannot execute the incompatible DML/DDL or transaction-control work in another container. Return to the transaction's originating container to finish it.

```sql
ALTER SESSION SET CONTAINER=PDB1;
COMMIT;
ALTER SESSION SET CONTAINER=PDB2;
```

This is one possible resolution if committing is intended; use ROLLBACK in PDB1 instead when the change must be abandoned. Never insert a COMMIT merely to make a training example proceed without considering its business effect. The alternatives that claim a cross-container switch commits, rolls back, or freely carries the same local transaction into PDB2 confuse a session attribute with transaction ownership. Verify CON_NAME before both data changes and transaction control in administrative scripts, particularly when a common-user connection switches among several tenants. The transaction remains associated with its original container until explicitly resolved there.

Core idea: Database point-in-time recovery (DBPITR) restores a database backup and applies redo only up to a chosen target. RMAN needs the database mounted so it can restore and recover files while they are not open for normal use. ARCHIVELOG mode preserves archived redo needed to advance from the backup to the target.

Option analysis: A is false; Flashback Database is a different recovery feature, not a DBPITR prerequisite. B is correct: perform the recovery with the database mounted. C is false; MRP is used for managed standby recovery, not primary RMAN DBPITR. D is correct because archived redo is required. E is potentially ambiguous: RMAN supports time, SCN, and log-sequence targets, so the listed choices are not exhaustive. F is correct: after incomplete recovery, open with RESETLOGS to start a new database incarnation.

Practical point: identify the target precisely and ensure all redo needed through it is available before starting.

For example, an RMAN recovery plan can select a target before restoring:
```text
RUN {
  SET UNTIL SCN 5000000;
  RESTORE DATABASE;
  RECOVER DATABASE;
}
```
The database must already be mounted and the selected backups must precede the target. RESETLOGS is a separate opening step after successful recovery. Do not infer a requirement to have generated flashback logs: this procedure applies archived redo to restored data files. A restore point is another usable target identifier, which is why Option E's two-item list is not exhaustive.
